The `if-else` statement allows conditional execution of code blocks. It executes a block if a condition is true, and optionally another block if false. Syntax:
```c
if (condition) {
    // code to execute if condition is true
} else {
    // code to execute if condition is false
}
```

Multiple conditions can be combined using logical operators. The two primary operators are:
*   Logical AND (`&&`): Returns true if *both* operands are true.
*   Logical OR (`||`): Returns true if *at least one* operand is true.

Example:
```c
if (age >= 18 && hasLicense) {
    // code
}
```

Fall-through occurs when, after a `case` block is executed, control proceeds to the next `case` block (or `default`) without checking its condition. This happens if `break` is omitted. To prevent fall-through, explicitly use the `break;` statement at the end of each `case` block you want to terminate:
```c
switch (val) {
    case 1: printf("One"); break;
    case 2: printf("Two"); // Fall-through if no break
    default: printf("Other");
}
```

The `for` loop header has three optional expressions: `for (initialization; condition; increment/decrement)`.
1.  `initialization`: executed once before the loop starts.
2.  `condition`: checked before each iteration; loop continues if true.
3.  `increment/decrement`: executed after each iteration.
Common use case: iterating a fixed number of times, e.g.:
```c
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // Loop body executes 10 times
}
```
